# leak-denylist — what must never reach a public repository
#
# COPY THIS FILE OUT OF THE REPOSITORY BEFORE YOU FILL IT IN:
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.config/vibeforge
#   cp leak-denylist.example ~/.config/vibeforge/leak-denylist
#
# A deny list committed to a repository publishes exactly what it lists. Both
# tools that read it refuse a list kept inside the repository they check.
#
# Read by:
#   vibeforge-gate               denylist-scan: ~/.config/vibeforge/leak-denylist,
#                                $VF_LEAK_DENYLIST, or newline-separated
#                                $VF_LEAK_DENYLIST_PATTERNS (agent environments)
#   repo-publication-readiness   sweep-leaks.py: the same three, plus its own
#                                --denylist, $RPR_DENYLIST and
#                                ~/.config/repo-publication-readiness/denylist
#
# FORMAT. One extended regular expression per line, matched case-insensitively.
# A line starting with '#' is a comment. Write in the subset that BOTH grep -E
# and Python's re understand:
#
#   [0-9] not \d       [a-z0-9_] not \w      (^|[^a-z0-9]) not \b
#   no lookarounds     no (?i)               no {,n} without a lower bound
#
# Write a literal dot as [.] and a word separator as [ _-]. [.] cannot drift
# into "any character", [ _-] also catches kebab-case and snake_case
# spellings, and together they keep a pattern from matching its own text when
# a copy of this file passes through a scan.
#
# Everything active below is generic and safe to share. Section 1 is the part
# that matters most, and only you can write it.

# --- 1. Your organizations — FILL IN: uncomment, then replace the placeholders
#
# Your employer: legal name, short name, ticker or abbreviation, email and
# web domains.
# example[ _-]?corp(oration)?
# (^|[^a-z0-9])excorp([^a-z0-9]|$)
# @example-corp[.]com
#
# Clients, past and present, by every name your team uses for them.
# client[ _-]?one
# client[ _-]?two
#
# Internal product, project and programme codenames. Anchor short or common
# words so they do not fire on ordinary prose.
# (^|[^a-z0-9])bluebird([^a-z0-9]|$)
#
# Internal hostnames that are not under an internal-only suffix (the gate's
# leak-scan already catches .corp, .internal, .lan and friends).
# [a-z0-9-]+[.]corp-example[.]com

# --- 2. Confidentiality markings
# Text pasted out of a document that was never meant to leave the building.
(strictly|company|highly)[ _-]confidential
proprietary[ _-](and|&)[ _-]confidential
privileged[ _-](and|&)[ _-]confidential
internal[ _-]use[ _-]only
for[ _-]internal[ _-](use|distribution)
not[ _-]for[ _-](public[ _-])?(distribution|release|disclosure)
do[ _-]not[ _-](distribute|forward|disclose)
attorney[ _-]client[ _-]privilege
(subject[ _-]to|covered[ _-]by|under)[ _-](an[ _-])?(nda|non[ _-]disclosure)
(contains|includes|constitutes)[ _-](confidential[ _-])?trade[ _-]secrets?
embargoed[ _-](until|till)

# --- 3. SaaS tenants whose hostname is the customer's name
# A tenant URL in a public repository names the organization that owns it.
# Delete a line if your public work legitimately shows that platform, and add
# the platforms you actually work in.
[a-z0-9-]+[.]service-now[.]com
[a-z0-9-]+[.]servicenowservices[.]com
[a-z0-9-]+[.](my[.]salesforce|lightning[.]force|my[.]site)[.]com
[a-z0-9-]+[.]atlassian[.]net
[a-z0-9-]+[.]sharepoint[.]com
[a-z0-9-]+[.]onmicrosoft[.]com
[a-z0-9-]+[.]snowflakecomputing[.]com
dev[.]azure[.]com/[a-z0-9-]+

# --- 4. Cloud account identifiers
# Not credentials, but each one maps a piece of someone's estate: an AWS ARN
# carries a 12-digit account id, an Azure resource path a subscription id.
arn:aws[a-z-]*:[a-z0-9-]+:[a-z0-9-]*:[0-9]{12}:
/subscriptions/[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}

# --- 5. Record numbers from systems of record — uncomment if you work in one
# ServiceNow's default numbering. A real incident or change number in a public
# repository ties it to the customer whose instance issued it.
# (^|[^a-z0-9])(inc|chg|prb|ritm|req|sctask|task)[0-9]{7}([^a-z0-9]|$)
